
{"id":"9e146c5c-c0c7-4e71-95c5-8183196a2b2b","object":"chat.completion","created":1785009074,"model":"deepseek-v4-flash","choices":[{"index":0,"message":{"role":"assistant","content":"{\n  \"implementation\": \"def solve(case):\\n    import sys\\n\\n    # Validate required top-level fields\\n    for key in ('min_cluster_size', 'clusters', 'parent'):\\n        if key not in case:\\n            raise ValueError(f'Missing required field: {key}')\\n    min_size = case['min_cluster_size']\\n    if not isinstance(min_size, int):\\n        raise ValueError('min_cluster_size must be an integer')\\n    clusters_in = case['clusters']\\n    if not isinstance(clusters_in, list):\\n        raise ValueError('clusters must be a list')\\n    parent = case['parent']\\n    if not isinstance(parent, dict):\\n        raise ValueError('parent must be a dict')\\n\\n    # Build cluster map and validate each cluster\\n    cluster_map = {}\\n    for c in clusters_in:\\n        if not isinstance(c, dict):\\n            raise ValueError('each cluster must be a dict')\\n        for key2 in ('cluster_id', 'size', 'birth_lambda', 'point_exit_lambdas'):\\n            if key2 not in c:\\n                raise ValueError(f'cluster missing field: {key2}')\\n        cid = c['cluster_id']\\n        if not isinstance(cid, str):\\n            raise ValueError('cluster_id must be a string')\\n        sz = c['size']\\n        if not isinstance(sz, int):\\n            raise ValueError('size must be an integer')\\n        bl = c['birth_lambda']\\n        if not isinstance(bl, (int, float)):\\n            raise ValueError('birth_lambda must be a number')\\n        exits = c['point_exit_lambdas']\\n        if not isinstance(exits, list):\\n            raise ValueError('point_exit_lambdas must be a list')\\n        for e in exits:\\n            if not isinstance(e, (int, float)):\\n                raise ValueError('exit lambda must be a number')\\n        cluster_map[cid] = c\\n\\n    # Validate parent keys cover all cluster ids\\n    for cid in cluster_map:\\n        if cid not in parent:\\n            raise ValueError(f'cluster {cid} not in parent mapping')\\n    for pid, pval in parent.items():\\n        if pval is not None and pval not in cluster_map:\\n            raise ValueError(f'parent value {pval} is not a valid cluster id')\\n\\n    # Filter clusters by min_cluster_size\\n    retained = {}\\n    for cid, c in cluster_map.items():\\n        if c['size'] >= min_size:\\n            retained[cid] = c\\n\\n    # Compute stability for each retained cluster\\n    stabilities = {}\\n    for cid, c in retained.items():\\n        birth = c['birth_lambda']\\n        total = 0.0\\n        for exit_lambda in c['point_exit_lambdas']:\\n            total += max(0.0, exit_lambda - birth)\\n        stabilities[cid] = total\\n\\n    # Build tree for retained clusters\\n    children = {cid: [] for cid in retained}\\n    roots = []\\n    for cid, c in retained.items():\\n        p = parent[cid]\\n        if p is None or p not in retained:\\n            roots.append(cid)\\n        else:\\n            children[p].append(cid)\\n\\n    # Prepare ancestor lists (only within retained)\\n    ancestor_of = {cid: [] for cid in retained}\\n    for cid in retained:\\n        anc = []\\n        cur = parent[cid]\\n        while cur is not None and cur in retained:\\n            anc.append(cur)\\n            cur = parent[cur]\\n        ancestor_of[cid] = anc\\n\\n    # Precompute descendant sets recursively\\n    descendants = {cid: set() for cid in retained}\\n    def compute_descendants(node):\\n        for ch in children[node]:\\n            compute_descendants(ch)\\n            descendants[node].add(ch)\\n            descendants[node].update(descendants[ch])\\n    for r in roots:\\n        compute_descendants(r)\\n\\n    # Sort retained clusters by (-stability, cluster_id)\\n    order = sorted(retained.keys(), key=lambda x: (-stabilities[x], x))\\n\\n    selected = set()\\n    blocked = set()\\n    for cid in order:\\n        if cid in blocked or cid in selected:\\n            continue\\n        # Check if any descendant is already selected\\n        already_selected_descendant = any(d in selected for d in descendants[cid])\\n        if already_selected_descendant:\\n            continue\\n        # Select this cluster\\n        selected.add(cid)\\n        # Block all ancestors (including itself? exclude itself since already selected)\\n        for anc in ancestor_of[cid]:\\n            blocked.add(anc)\\n\\n    # Build output clusters list\\n    out_clusters = []\\n    for cid in sorted(retained.keys()):  # deterministic order\\n        c = retained[cid]\\n        out_clusters.append({\\n            'cluster_id': c['cluster_id'],\\n            'size': c['size'],\\n            'stability': stabilities[cid]\\n        })\\n\\n    out_selected = sorted(selected)\\n\\n    return {\\n        'clusters': out_clusters,\\n        'selected_cluster_ids': out_selected\\n    }\\n\"\n}","reasoning_content":"We need to implement solve(case) as per the task DATA-HDB-02. The case payload contains: min_cluster_size, clusters (list of objects with cluster_id, size, birth_lambda, point_exit_lambdas), and parent (dict mapping cluster_id to parent cluster_id or null). We need to condense the hierarchy: drop clusters smaller than min_cluster_size. Then for each retained cluster, compute stability: sum over points of max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda). For a cluster, its points are not explicitly listed; we have cluster itself with size, but exit lambdas per point? Actually point_exit_lambdas is a list of lambdas for each point? The description says \"over its points\". There's no explicit mapping of points to clusters. The cluster has size and point_exit_lambdas list. The length of point_exit_lambdas might correspond to the number of points? But size is given. Possibly each point in the cluster has an exit lambda from that cluster. The birth_lambda is the cluster's birth. So for each point, its contribution to stability is max(0, point_exit_lambda - birth_lambda). But we need sum over all points. The cluster's size indicates number of points, but point_exit_lambdas list may have fewer entries? In the fixture, cluster c0 size 7 and point_exit_lambdas length 3. That's inconsistent. Maybe point_exit_lambdas are the exit lambdas for points that actually leave (i.e., those that exit at some lambda) and the rest stay until infinity? Or maybe each point has an exit lambda that is the lambda at which it leaves the cluster. Typically in HDBSCAN, a point's exit lambda from a cluster is the lambda at which it leaves the cluster (which could be infinity if it never leaves). Here we have a list of exit lambdas; might be only the points that do exit (non-infinite). Points that never leave have exit lambda = infinity, and max(0, inf - birth) = inf, so stability infinite? Actually stability is sum of (lambda_p - lambda_birth) for points that exit (lambda_p > lambda_birth). But if a point stays forever, its exit lambda is infinite, contribution infinite -> infinite stability. That doesn't make sense.\n\nLet's re-read the instructions: \"For each retained cluster sum max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) over its points.\" That is standard condensed tree stability. In HDBSCAN, each point has a birth lambda (when it first appears) and a death lambda (when it disappears) for each cluster. For a given cluster, the birth lambda is cluster birth (when it appears), and each point has its own death lambda (when it leaves the cluster). The stability is sum over points of (death_lambda - birth_lambda) for points where death_lambda > birth_lambda. If a point never leaves (death at infinity), its stability contribution is infinite? Actually in HDBSCAN, infinite stability is avoided by considering infinity as a large value? But in practice, points that stay till the end are part of the root cluster. The condensed tree includes points that exit. The cluster's sta