his cluster (including those that exit and those that stay). The points that stay are not in the list, so their exit lambda is effectively infinite. But computing stability as sum of max(0, exit - birth) for points with exit, plus for points without exit? If a point never exits, exit is infinite, max(0, inf - birth) = inf, so stability infinite. That would make infinite stability clusters unselectable? Actually infinite stability clusters would always be selected because they are more stable. That would break the algorithm.\n\nLet's think about the HDBSCAN condensed tree: Each cluster in the condensed tree has a \"lambda_birth\" and \"lambda_death\" (or just birth). Each point has a \"lambda_p\" that is the lambda at which it leaves the cluster (its death). For clusters that are not the root, points that stay till the end of the cluster (i.e., until the cluster itself dies) are considered to have exit lambda equal to the cluster's death lambda? Actually, the cluster dies when its last point leaves, so for points that never leave, they would be part of the cluster until it merges into a larger cluster or disappears. In condensed tree, clusters are defined as connected components at a given lambda. The cluster's birth is when it first appears, and its death is when it disappears (hierarchy). Points have their own birth and death. For a given cluster, points that are part of it have death lambda >= cluster death? The point may leave the cluster before the cluster dies. The stability of a cluster is sum over points of (lambda_p - lambda_birth) for points that have lambda_p > lambda_birth. This includes points that exit the cluster at lambda_p. But points that stay until the cluster dies have lambda_p = lambda_cluster_death (which is the lambda at which the cluster disappears, usually because it merges with another cluster). In the condensed tree representation, the cluster's death is the lambda at which it splits or merges. The stability is computed using each point's lambda_p, which is the lambda at which the point leaves the cluster. For points that leave exactly at the cluster death, that's still a valid exit. So if a point never leaves (i.e., it's in a root cluster that never dies), then its exit lambda is infinite. But in practice, root cluster stability is not considered because it has no parent; the algorithm only selects clusters that are more stable than their descendants.\n\nIn the given payload, we have only birth_lambda for clusters, no death lambda. The parent mapping indicates hierarchy: a cluster's parent is the cluster it belongs to at higher lambda? Typically parent is the cluster that contains it at higher scale. So the cluster's death lambda is the birth lambda of its parent? Actually, in HDBSCAN, when a cluster splits, the parent cluster's death lambda is the birth lambda of its children? It's reversed: as lambda increases, clusters appear and disappear. Usually, as lambda increases, clusters grow, then at some lambda they split into children. So a cluster's birth is at a certain lambda, and its death is when it splits into children. The children have a birth lambda that is the same as the parent's death lambda. So the parent's death lambda is the children's birth lambda. Here we have parent mapping: c0 is parent of c1 and c2. So c0's death lambda is the birth lambda of c1 and c2? But c1 and c2 have different birth lambdas: c1 birth=0.5, c2 birth=0.8. So inconsistency: c0 would die at c1's birth (0.5) and at c2's birth (0.8)? That can't be. Actually, in a hierarchy, a parent cluster can spawn multiple children at different lambdas. So the parent cluster's death is the minimum of its children's birth lambdas? Typically, a cluster persists until it splits into at least two clusters. The first split at lambda 0.5 produces c1, but c0 still continues with remaining points until it splits again at 0.8 to produce c2, then c0 dies. So c0's death lambda is 0.8 (the last split). So the parent's death lambda is the maximu