ady selected descendant is blocking? Actually: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" Typically, in condensed tree selection, we traverse in order of decreasing stability, and if a cluster has no descendants selected that have higher stability? The standard algorithm: start from leaves, compute stability, then propagate up: if a cluster's stability is greater than the sum of its children's stabilities, then select that cluster and remove its descendants; else, keep the children. But here the description: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" This suggests we process clusters in decreasing stability, and for each cluster, if any of its descendants are already selected, we skip it (because the descendant is more stable and blocks it). If no selected descendant, we select it and then block its ancestors (i.e., mark them as blocked so they cannot be selected later). That is similar to the standard selection.\n\nSo we need to build the tree from parent mapping. Identify root(s). For each cluster, find its children (clusters that have this cluster as parent). Compute stabilities as described. Then we need to sort retained clusters by decreasing stability, and for ties, we need deterministic tie handling: \"Rank clusters by decreasing stability and cluster ID.\" So for equal stability, use cluster ID string comparison (probably ascending).\n\nThen perform selection: Iterate through sorted list. For each cluster, check if any of its descendants (in the tree) are already selected. If yes, skip. Otherwise, select it, and then mark all its ancestors as blocked (meaning they cannot be selected later). We can implement by maintaining a set of selected_cluster_ids and a set of blocked clusters (ancestors of selected ones). Or we can mark ancestors as blocked during selection.\n\nThe output: \"Return all retained stability rows and sorted selected IDs.\" So we need to output an object with \"clusters\" array containing each retained cluster (with cluster_id, size, stability) and \"selected_cluster_ids\" array sorted (by cluster ID ascending? Should be the order of selection? Probably ascending cluster IDs). The description: \"sorted selected IDs\" suggests ascending order.\n\nLet's confirm with given fixture. For case a-000, we have min_cluster_size=3. So retain c0 (size7) and c1 (size4). c2 dropped. Stabilities computed as above. Now tree: root c0 has children c1 and c2 (but c2 is dropped, so we ignore it in tree for selection? We should consider only retained clusters. The hierarchy still matters: c0 is ancestor of c1. So tree: c0 parent of c1. Stabilities: c1=1.4, c0=1.3. Sorted: c1 first, then c0. Process c1: check descendants of c1 (none, since no children retained). No selected descendant, so select c1. Then block ancestors: ancestor c0 is blocked. Then process c0: check selected descendants? c0 has descendant c1 selected, so c0 is blocked. So selected = [c1]. Output: clusters: both c0 and c1 with stabilities. selected_cluster_ids: [\"c1\"] sorted (already). That seems plausible.\n\nBut we must ensure we handle all clusters, even those dropped. The output includes only retained clusters (those with size >= min_cluster_size). And we compute stability for them using only their point_exit_lambdas.\n\nNow we must implement solve(case) that returns dict with \"clusters\" list and \"selected_cluster_ids\". Use only standard lib.\n\nEdge cases: If no clusters remain after filtering, return empty arrays. If multiple roots? Possibly.\n\nWe need to validate required fields: case must have min_cluster_size (int), clusters (list), parent (dict). Each cluster must have cluster_id (string), size (int), birth_lambda (number), point_exit_lambdas (list of numbers). Parent mapping must be consistent: all cluster_ids in clusters must appear as keys in parent, parent values are either null or one of the cluster_ids (not necessarily retained). If not, raise ValueError.\n\nWe must not mutate case.\n\nImplementation steps:\n1. Validate case structure (fields present, types). Raise ValueError if missing or invalid.\n2. Extract min_cluster_size.\n3. Build dict of clusters by id: cluster_map = {c['cluster_id']: c for c in case['clusters']}\n4. Validate parent mapping keys: all cluster ids in clusters must be in parent dict keys. Also each parent value is either None or a string that is a key in parent (or cluster_map?) - but it could be a cluster that is not in the input? Typically parent refers to another cluster in the same set, so we can assume it's present. Check that all parent values (if not None) are in cluster_map.\n5. Filter clusters: retain only those with size >= min_cluster_size. Store in a dict retained = {id: cluster}\n6. For each retained cluster, compute stability: sum(max(0, exit - birth) for exit in cluster['point_exit_lambdas']). Use float. Note: If point_exit_lambdas is empty, stability = 0.\n7. Build tree for retained clusters: find children for each retained cluster. For each cluster in retained, look at parent mapping. If parent is not None and parent is in retained, then add this cluster as child of parent. Also identify roots: clusters whose parent is None or parent not in retained.\n8. Sort retained clusters by (-stability, cluster_id) for selection order.\n9. Perform selection:\n   - selected = set()\n   - blocked = set() (ancestors that are already blocked)\n   - For each cluster in order:\n       - if cluster_id in blocked: continue (should not happen if we block ancestors on selection, but we can still check)\n       - Check if any descendant (including indirect) is already selected. To check, we need to traverse descendants. We can precompute descendant sets for each cluster via DFS on the tree (only retained clusters). Or while iterating, we can maintain selected set and for each candidate, traverse its descendant subtree to see if any selected. That's O(n^2) but fine for